WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.699 --> 00:00:04.282
[angelic orchestral music]

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.180
My name is Sara, and I
am an art conservator

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.690
at Center Art Studio.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.660
I am going to walk you
through how my team and I

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:22.140
restore a 19th-century
Italian marble sculpture

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.130
and its alabaster base.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.496
So it's been broken,

00:00:25.496 --> 00:00:26.654
it's been previously repaired.

00:00:26.654 --> 00:00:29.120
It came to us broken again.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.878
This work of art is comprised

00:00:31.878 --> 00:00:34.600
of four separate pieces.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.985
That includes the marble bust,

00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:40.643
its socle base, and a pedestal,

00:00:40.643 --> 00:00:43.726
which is in two parts, a top and a column.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.730
We're going to clean the
bust, the socle base,

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.390
and the pedestal
components all separately.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:55.470
We start out on all of the pieces

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.578
by using a surfactant detergent

00:00:58.578 --> 00:01:01.319
diluted in distilled water

00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:05.110
and spray it onto the
marble and the alabaster.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:06.960
We want to use what we need

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.984
to properly clean a piece,

00:01:08.984 --> 00:01:11.434
but we never want to do anything

00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:12.767
that's too harsh.

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:15.590
And this surfactant is very gentle,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.920
and it lifts the dirt off the surface,

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:20.380
kind of breaks the surface tension

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.230
between the dirt and the surface,

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:26.804
and allows us to wipe or swab away

00:01:26.804 --> 00:01:30.600
the accumulated surface dirt and debris.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.483
The column is quite dirty,

00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:37.125
and this is because a pedestal column

00:01:37.125 --> 00:01:38.382
sits on the floor

00:01:38.382 --> 00:01:41.600
and the dirt from the
floor, from people's shoes,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.550
it all gets brought up onto that surface.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.880
We also use surfactant gels,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.426
particularly in areas that might be

00:01:51.426 --> 00:01:53.373
a little bit more stubborn to clean.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.300
When restoring something so old,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.290
you have to be mindful
of the age of the piece.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:05.090
So we never want to remove all of the dirt

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.720
out of all of the recesses

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.790
and completely remove the patina

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:13.270
that's built up on an item of age

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:14.530
because that would end up

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.483
looking incorrect and inappropriate.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.360
So the top of the pedestal
and the socle base

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.330
that the bust sits directly on

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.000
have both been previously repaired twice

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.170
with the same types of glue.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:35.661
We need to entirely remove

00:02:35.661 --> 00:02:37.778
all of this old adhesive

00:02:37.778 --> 00:02:40.430
that's not functional, it's yellowing,

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.280
it's not good for the marble

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.896
to have on its surface.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:45.490
So we want to make sure
we get all of it off.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:50.490
So we are using a combination of scalpels.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.091
This is also a time

00:02:52.091 --> 00:02:53.300
where you have to be very careful

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.830
because marble is very susceptible

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.938
and alabaster is very, very susceptible

00:02:57.938 --> 00:02:58.779
to scratching.

00:02:58.779 --> 00:03:01.190
So when you're using
those very sharp tools

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.558
you want to make sure

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:05.964
that you are only scraping off the glue

00:03:05.964 --> 00:03:08.460
and you're not accidentally nicking

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.513
the surrounding surface area.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.430
We use a number of visual aids to help us

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.450
in the conservation process.

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:25.266
One tool that's very helpful

00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:27.660
is a small spotlight.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.200
This is a very direct, targeted light

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.210
that allows us to very
clearly see the area

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.702
that we are working on.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.970
We also use a black light,
which is very useful

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.550
because the UV light

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:45.841
allows us to see imperfections

00:03:45.841 --> 00:03:48.008
and residues on the surface

00:03:48.008 --> 00:03:52.652
that are not visible to
the regular human eye.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.920
To attach the broken items back together,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.310
we are going to use a
special conservation epoxy.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:10.293
It is called Epo-Tek.
This is a two-part epoxy

00:04:10.293 --> 00:04:11.290
that you combine together.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:14.650
When you first mix it,
it can be quite liquidy,

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.863
so we allow it to sit
for a little bit of time

00:04:18.863 --> 00:04:20.763
just to develop little bit more

00:04:20.763 --> 00:04:22.538
of a viscous quality.

00:04:23.488 --> 00:04:28.300
We are going to permanently
attach the marble

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.740
to the socle base.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.230
We're going to reuse
the original metal rod.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.500
Once we have it at the right depth,

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.031
we put the pin in the hole of the bust

00:04:40.031 --> 00:04:44.984
and we use our epoxy,
pour that into the recess.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.410
And then we put the base on the marble,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.243
check it for alignment,

00:05:01.243 --> 00:05:03.226
front to back and left to right,

00:05:03.226 --> 00:05:07.701
and allow that to cure for a little while.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.665
To ensure that the repair

00:05:11.665 --> 00:05:14.215
of the top of the pedestal
is extremely strong,

00:05:14.215 --> 00:05:17.630
we are going to pin the pieces

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.000
in addition to epoxying them.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.520
We are going to take
a stainless-steel rod.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.640
Stainless steel is desirable

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.390
because it's not going to rust

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.620
or change over time.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.730
We mark on the outside of the piece

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:36.713
where we're going to drill holes

00:05:36.713 --> 00:05:38.796
to take the pins.

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:42.233
And we are using our ancient drill press.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.630
Once we have the holes, we have the pins,

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:55.700
we then can use our epoxy

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:59.536
to attach the two pieces back together.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.793
We are going to fill the cracks

00:06:13.793 --> 00:06:16.543
in the sculpture with plaster.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.463
We are applying the plaster

00:06:23.463 --> 00:06:25.329
with small metal tools.

00:06:25.329 --> 00:06:28.770
We tend to err on the side of overfilling

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.495
because it's very easy

00:06:30.495 --> 00:06:33.161
to remove the plaster material.

00:06:33.161 --> 00:06:35.159
The plaster cures quickly, so you want

00:06:35.159 --> 00:06:37.230
to make sure that you're getting enough on

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.206
while it's still workable.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:43.330
Once we've filled the cracks

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.860
and we've allowed the
plaster to fully dry,

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.570
we are going to sand,

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:53.770
particularly the alabaster base,

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.679
which had a large crack

00:06:55.679 --> 00:06:56.769
throughout the entire thing.

00:06:56.769 --> 00:06:58.800
We are going to sand that down

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.624
with different grits of sandpaper.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.068
In painting, a marble sculpture

00:07:12.068 --> 00:07:14.593
is really the same technique that we use

00:07:14.593 --> 00:07:17.730
for any other sculpture or a painting.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:21.780
We are using our standard
Gamblin Conservation Colors.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.530
We mix them up to get the right color.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:25.860
In this instance,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.560
because the marble and the alabaster

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.550
are sort of translucent,

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.200
it makes our job a little trickier

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.760
because we're trying to
replicate translucency

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.770
on an opaque fill.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.300
In this instance, you
really can't mix one color

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.860
and apply it to the whole fill.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.780
You have to do thin
washes of different colors

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:48.480
to mimic the texture and the veining

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.020
of the stone material.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:54.670
We are going to polish the alabaster base,

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:58.460
and we use a very large
polishing wheel for this.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:02.830
And we use polishing compound
applied to the wheel,

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.438
and that polishing compound comes

00:08:05.438 --> 00:08:06.846
in a variety of grits.

00:08:06.846 --> 00:08:09.650
We use very fine ones in this instance

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:11.913
so it doesn't damage the alabaster.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:17.620
The final step of the conservation

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:22.620
is to wax each piece of the sculpture.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:24.826
So we are using

00:08:24.826 --> 00:08:28.234
a very, very fine microcrystalline wax.

00:08:28.234 --> 00:08:30.690
And we apply this

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:34.460
over the whole surface of
each one of the pieces,

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.390
allow it to dry for just a little bit,

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.690
and then buff it off very gently

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.881
with soft cloths.

00:08:40.881 --> 00:08:42.518
This wax is important.

00:08:42.518 --> 00:08:47.518
It not only makes the
stone look good visually,

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:50.830
it also serves as a protective layer

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:54.760
between the stone and the elements.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.493
Once all of the components

00:08:56.493 --> 00:08:58.684
are complete in their own right,

00:08:58.684 --> 00:09:01.070
we do a final assembly, and this is how

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.940
the piece will be ultimately displayed

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.453
at the client's home.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:15.500
We are very satisfied

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:17.530
with how the whole piece came out.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:19.830
We know the client loves this piece

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:23.070
and has had it broken for a long time.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.004
We think she's going be very happy.

00:09:25.004 --> 00:09:28.171
[bright, jaunty music]

